L["cornelius"] = """In the city of Caesarea lived a Roman army officer named Cornelius. He was a centurion, in charge of about a hundred soldiers. He was not Jewish, but he was devout. He and all his family feared God. He gave generously to those in need and prayed to God regularly.

One afternoon, Cornelius had a vision. He saw an angel of God, who said to him, Cornelius! Your prayers and gifts to the poor have come up as a memorial offering before God. Now send men to Joppa to bring back a man named Simon who is called Peter. He is staying with Simon the tanner, whose house is by the sea. Cornelius sent two servants and a soldier to find him.

The next day, as they approached Joppa, Peter went up on the roof to pray. He was hungry, and while the meal was being prepared, he fell into a trance. He saw heaven opened and something like a large sheet being let down to earth by its four corners. It contained all kinds of animals, including some that the Jewish law said were unclean. Then a voice told him, get up, Peter. Kill and eat. Surely not, Lord! Peter replied. I have never eaten anything impure or unclean. The voice spoke to him a second time. Do not call anything impure that God has made clean. This happened three times, and immediately the sheet was taken back to heaven.

While Peter was wondering what the vision meant, the men sent by Cornelius arrived. The Spirit told him, simon, three men are looking for you. Go with them, for I have sent them. Peter went with them to Caesarea. Cornelius had gathered his relatives and close friends. When Peter entered the house, Cornelius fell at his feet in reverence. Peter made him get up. Stand up, I am only a man myself.

Then Peter said, you are well aware that it is against our law for a Jew to associate with or visit a Gentile. But God has shown me that I should not call anyone impure or unclean. So when I was sent for, I came without raising any objection. May I ask why you sent for me? Cornelius explained. Then Peter began to speak. I now realize how true it is that God does not show favoritism, but accepts from every nation the one who fears him and does what is right.

He told them about Jesus, who had gone about doing good and healing, whom God raised from the dead. While Peter was still speaking, the Holy Spirit came on all who heard the message. The Jewish believers who had come with Peter were astonished that the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out even on Gentiles. So Peter ordered that they be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ.

Why does this matter? This is the moment the gospel crossed the line from the Jewish world to the whole world. It was a huge change. God was making it clear that his love was for everyone.

What happened after? Peter went back to Jerusalem, and some believers criticized him for eating with Gentiles. He explained everything. When they heard it, they praised God and said, so then, even to Gentiles God has granted repentance that leads to life.

What does this mean for us today? We all have people we think are outside. God invites us to see them differently. It also shows how God arranges meetings. He spoke to Cornelius and to Peter at the same time, so each was ready for the other. And it reminds us that most of us are descendants of this moment. The gospel reached us because Peter said yes.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God shows no favoritism, and that the good news of Jesus is for every nation."""
